Certainly! To force a textfield to display columns in Adobe JavaScript, you can use the `columns` property of the textfield object. Here's a simple example of how you can do this:

```javascript
// Assuming you have a textfield named "myTextField"
var myTextField = getField("myTextField");

// Set the number of columns to 20
myTextField.columns = 20;
```

In this example, `getField("myTextField")` retrieves the textfield with the name "myTextField". Then, the `columns` property is set to 20, which will make the textfield display 20 columns.

If you need to set this up in a more complex scenario, such as within a script triggered by an event, you can do so like this:

```javascript
// Assuming the textfield is named "myTextField" and the event is triggered by a button click
var myTextField = getField("myTextField");

// Set the number of columns to 20 when the button is clicked
on("buttonClicked", function() {
    myTextField.columns = 20;
});
```

This script will set the number of columns to 20 whenever the button with the name "buttonClicked" is clicked.

